+# Microsoft Fabric Lakehouse (Spark)
+
+Bruin supports Microsoft Fabric **Lakehouses** with Spark SQL as the compute
+engine, including running PySpark when needed. Assets execute inside a Fabric
+Spark session through the lakehouse's [Livy API](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/api-livy-overview),
+materializing Delta tables natively.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> This platform targets the Lakehouse Spark engine. For Fabric
+> **Warehouses** (T-SQL over the SQL endpoint), use the
+> [`fabric`](./fabric.md) platform instead.
+
+## Connection
+
+```yaml
+connections:
+  fabric_spark:
+    - name: "connection_name"
+      workspace_id: "fabric-workspace-guid"
+      lakehouse_id: "lakehouse-item-guid"
+      lakehouse_name: "my_lakehouse"
+
+      # Option A — service principal (recommended for CI):
+      tenant_id: "azure-tenant-guid"
+      client_id: "app-registration-client-id"
+      client_secret: "app-registration-secret"
+
+      # Option B — pre-acquired token (local development):
+      # access_token: "eyJ..."
+      # e.g. az account get-access-token --scope "https://analysis.windows.net/powerbi/api/.default"
+```
+
+The service principal needs Contributor (or higher) access to the workspace,
+and the tenant must allow service principals to use Fabric APIs.
+
+Optional fields:
+
+| Field | Description |
+|-------|-------------|
+| `schema` | Default schema for schema-enabled lakehouses (e.g. `dbo`) |
+| `workspace_name` | Workspace display name; enables ingestr integration (OneLake destination) |
+| `endpoint` | Fabric API base URL, defaults to `https://api.fabric.microsoft.com/v1` |
+| `session_name` | Spark session name shown in the Fabric monitoring UI |
+| `environment_id` | Pin sessions to a Fabric Environment (custom pool/libraries) |
+| `spark_config` | Extra Spark conf key/values for the session |
+| `high_concurrency` | Run parallel assets concurrently in one Spark app (default `false`) |
+| `session_tag` | REPL packing key for high-concurrency mode |
+| `max_concurrent_repls` | REPL pool cap in high-concurrency mode (default 4) |
+| `session_start_timeout_seconds` | Session cold-start budget (default 600) |
+| `statement_timeout_seconds` | Per-statement budget (default 43200; 0 = unlimited) |
+
+## Assets
+
+### `fabric.spark.sql`
+
+Runs Spark SQL against the lakehouse with full materialization support:
+
+```bruin-sql
+/* @bruin
+name: reporting.daily_counts
+type: fabric.spark.sql
+materialization:
+  type: table
+  strategy: merge
+columns:
+  - name: event_date
+    type: date
+    primary_key: true
+  - name: event_count
+    type: bigint
+    update_on_merge: true
+@bruin */
+
+SELECT event_date, COUNT(*) AS event_count
+FROM raw.events
+GROUP BY event_date
+```
+
+Supported strategies: `create+replace` (with `partition_by` or `cluster_by`),
+`append`, `merge`, `delete+insert`, `truncate+insert`, `time_interval`,
+`ddl`, `scd2_by_column`, `scd2_by_time`, and `anti_join`.
+
+`anti_join` is an insert-only incremental: rows whose (possibly compound)
+business key — the asset's `primary_key` columns — already exist in the
+target are skipped via a null-safe `LEFT ANTI JOIN`, so re-runs never
+duplicate or update rows. Adding `incremental_key` + `time_granularity`
+bounds the target-side scan to the run window for cheap joins on large
+tables.
+
+The SCD2 strategies run as a single incremental `MERGE` and require Fabric
+runtime 1.3+ (Delta 3.x).
+
+### `fabric.spark.pyspark`
+
+The asset's Python file runs as a PySpark statement in the same Spark
+session as the SQL assets, with `spark` and `sc` predefined and the
+lakehouse as the default catalog:
+
+```bruin-python
+""" @bruin
+name: transform.enrich_events
+type: fabric.spark.pyspark
+depends:
+  - raw.events
+@bruin """
+
+from pyspark.sql import functions as F
+
+events = spark.table("raw.events")
+events.withColumn("processed_at", F.current_timestamp()) \
+    .write.mode("overwrite").saveAsTable("transform.enriched_events")
+```
+
+### `fabric.spark.seed`
+
+Loads a CSV next to the asset file into a Delta table, with types taken from
+the column definitions:
+
+```yaml
+name: raw.countries
+type: fabric.spark.seed
+parameters:
+  path: countries.csv
+```
+
+### Sensors
+
+`fabric.spark.sensor.query` pokes a SQL condition;
+`fabric.spark.sensor.table` waits for a table to exist (probed via
+`SHOW TABLES`, since lakehouses expose no `information_schema`).
+
+## Notes
+
+- One Livy session serves the connection by default; statements share temp
+  views and catalog state across SQL and PySpark assets. A cold Fabric
+  capacity can take minutes to start the session — this cost is paid once
+  per run.
+- With `high_concurrency: true`, the connection pools REPLs from Fabric's
+  `/highConcurrencySessions` API so parallel assets genuinely execute
+  concurrently inside one Spark application.
+- Quality checks are supported, including Spark-specific `accepted_values`
+  and `pattern` (via `RLIKE`).
